  <section class="ph-page-hero">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <div class="ph-eyebrow">Guide · Start here</div>
      <h1 class="ph-page-title">Getting Started</h1>
      <p class="ph-page-lede">A five-minute first-launch checklist. Set these once and PodHaven will feel like yours.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">1. Bring in your subscriptions</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">Two paths in:</p>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>OPML import.</strong> If you're coming from another podcast app, export your subscriptions there as OPML, then go to <em>Settings → Importing / Exporting → OPML → Import</em>. The whole library lands subscribed in seconds.</li>
        <li><strong>Search.</strong> Tap the magnifying glass at the bottom-right. The Search tab opens directly to Apple's Top Podcasts chart. Browse the eighteen genre chips, search by name, or paste an RSS feed via the <em>Manual Entry</em> option for indie shows.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="ph-tip">
        <strong>Pro tip:</strong> you can mix-and-match. Bring 80% of your library in via OPML, then use Search to fill the gaps. PodHaven dedupes by feed URL, so importing twice is harmless.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">2. Tune the basics</h2>
      <p class="ph-lede">Open <em>Settings</em> (gear icon at the bottom). The defaults are reasonable, but spend a minute on these:</p>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Default Playback Rate.</strong> Drag to your usual speed. 1.2× and 1.4× are popular. New episodes start at this rate.</li>
        <li><strong>Skip Forward / Backward.</strong> 30s forward, 15s back is the default and the right answer for most people. Bump forward to 45s if you skip ads frequently; drop back to 10s if you mostly use it for "wait, what did they just say?"</li>
        <li><strong>Next Track Behavior.</strong> Decide what your AirPods squeeze, lock-screen Next, or car steering-wheel button does: <em>Next Episode</em> (default), <em>Skip Interval</em>, or <em>Next Chapter</em>. Pick the one your hands already expect.</li>
        <li><strong>Enable Undo Seek.</strong> Highly recommended. When on, dragging the progress bar briefly turns the skip-back button into an Undo button. Saves you the day you palm the screen on a bumpy commute.</li>
        <li><strong>Cache Size Limit.</strong> Found under Storage. Pick a budget that fits how much offline listening you need — 2 GB is a sensible default, 5 GB if you commute long distances.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">3. Set up Up Next the way you actually listen</h2>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Max Queue Length.</strong> 25 is the sweet spot. Big enough to hold a week of listening, small enough to force you to triage.</li>
        <li><strong>Show Now Playing.</strong> When on, the currently-playing episode pins to the top of the queue. Most people prefer it on.</li>
        <li><strong>Recommended Episodes.</strong> Set how many recommendation picks show beneath your queue (0–20). Five is a good starting number.</li>
        <li><strong>Auto-play Top Recommendation.</strong> When the queue empties, PodHaven auto-plays the top pick from the rec engine. Turn it on if you'd rather never hit silence — off if you'd rather decide for yourself.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section ph-section-alt">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">4. Allow notifications (selectively)</h2>
      <p>iOS will ask for notification permission the first time PodHaven needs it. Allow it — but the global permission is just the door. Per-podcast notifications are off by default; turn them on only for the shows you actually want a ping for.</p>
      <ol class="ph-steps">
        <li>Open a subscribed podcast.</li>
        <li>Tap the settings icon in the top toolbar.</li>
        <li>Toggle <strong>Notify New Episodes</strong> on for that podcast.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Repeat for each podcast you want pings from. The daily news show that already lives at the top of your queue probably doesn't need notifications. The weekly investigative one you'd otherwise miss probably does.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ph-section">
    <div class="ph-container">
      <h2 class="ph-section-title">5. Train the recommendation engine</h2>
      <p>The rec engine starts knowing nothing. Give it ten ratings on episodes you've already listened to and it'll start surfacing real picks under your queue.</p>
      <ul class="ph-checklist ph-checklist-wide">
        <li><strong>Love</strong> the ones you'd recommend to a friend.</li>
        <li><strong>Like</strong> the ones you enjoyed.</li>
        <li><strong>Dislike</strong> the ones that wasted your time. (This is the rating that actually trains the model away from a topic.)</li>
        <li><strong>Not Interested</strong> to dismiss a specific episode from your recommendations and lists. Note: it's a view filter, not a training signal — use Dislike if you want the engine to learn.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Don't overthink it. Rate ten or fifteen episodes liberally, then let the queue start filling itself. <a class="ph-inline-link" href="/podhaven/guides/training-recommendations">Read the training guide for tactics →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

